cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use BLACKSTART in academic or professional work, please cite it."
title: "BLACKSTART: A Consequence-Driven Cyber-Physical Resilience Range for Critical Infrastructure Under Compromise"
abstract: >-
  BLACKSTART is a reproducible cyber-physical research environment for measuring
  whether essential infrastructure functions can remain safe, observable,
  recoverable and operational during sophisticated cyber compromise. It models a
  fictional municipal water storage and pumping process as a deterministic
  digital twin, evaluates explicit safety invariants against ground-truth
  physical state, derives consequence severity from measurable conditions, and
  compares outcomes with and without a simulated independent engineering
  constraint. All infrastructure and parameters are synthetic. The environment
  contains no exploitation tooling; adversarial conditions are represented as
  bounded, declarative simulation effects.
type: software
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-24"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/bbrookhart/blackstart-cyber-range"

# Author identity is not recorded in this repository. Replace this placeholder
# with real author metadata before citing or publishing. A fabricated author,
# affiliation or ORCID would be worse than an obvious placeholder.
authors:
  - name: "BLACKSTART Research"
    # Replace with real author metadata before citing.

keywords:
  - industrial control systems
  - operational technology
  - cyber-physical systems
  - critical infrastructure
  - consequence-driven engineering
  - cyber range
  - resilience
  - reproducible research

references:
  - type: report
    title: "Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security"
    abbreviation: "NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    year: 2023
    notes: >-
      Used as the principal OT security reference for architecture and risk
      considerations. BLACKSTART claims no conformance or certification.
  - type: report
    title: "The Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0"
    abbreviation: "NIST CSF 2.0"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    year: 2024
    notes: >-
      Outcomes are mapped for orientation only. No certification is claimed.
  - type: report
    title: "Incident Response Recommendations and Considerations for Cybersecurity Risk Management"
    abbreviation: "NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    year: 2025
  - type: database
    title: "MITRE ATT&CK for ICS"
    institution:
      name: "The MITRE Corporation"
    url: "https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/ics/"
    notes: >-
      Technique mappings were verified against attack.mitre.org on 2026-08-24
      (ATT&CK v19.2). Mappings are made at the level of simulated effect and are
      recorded with rationale in framework-mappings/attack-ics.yaml.
  - type: generic
    title: "Consequence-driven Cyber-informed Engineering (CCE)"
    institution:
      name: "Idaho National Laboratory"
    notes: >-
      BLACKSTART's consequence analysis is INFORMED BY consequence-driven
      engineering principles. It is not an implementation of CCE, and no
      endorsement or affiliation is implied.
